[
  {
    "prompt": "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. The composition is centered with generous negative space, presented at a 3/4 overhead angle (40°) to showcase the dish volume while keeping all components clearly visible. Two matte ceramic plates are arranged side-by-side, one sharp in the foreground and one softly blurred in the background. Each plate features a generous mound of fluffy, light-brown whole wheat couscous as a base. Beside the couscous, three golden-brown, pan-seared pork medallions with visible thyme flecks and spice crust rest next to a portion of bright green, 2-inch chopped asparagus segments. The meat and vegetables are separately identifiable, not stacked. A rich, glossy, amber-toned balsamic-mustard glaze clings to the pork and asparagus, with a small pool of sauce pooled near the couscous. Surface details include glistening oil droplets and subtle, caramelized brown bits from the skillet. Lighting is soft, natural side light from a window on the left, casting gentle, diffused shadows. The setting is a rustic, aged oak tabletop with visible wood grain. Props include a wooden spatula resting casually on the table, a slightly rumpled, off-white linen napkin tucked under one plate, and a few stray grains of couscous and a single loose piece of asparagus on the surface. Photorealistic, not AI-generated looking. The scene reflects a home-cooked, lived-in meal. Slight natural imperfections: an asymmetric garnish of fresh herbs, a tiny balsamic glaze drip on the rim, and uneven searing on the pork. No plastic sheen, no artificial studio lighting, no symmetry. Warm, golden-hour color temperature. No text, no watermark, no faces, no hands. Indistinguishable from a professional cookbook photograph."
  }
]
